  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "What is the maximum value of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $1$ unit **up.**\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/54d318d07c577282a73a51bafb9262060cef8e3a4cf04d726a0abb0a471252e4.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that  $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nAdding $1$ to both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1\\le 1+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1$ is $2.$",
